= Wave 1: The Opportunist — Strategy Guide

== Advanced Trading & Production Chains

By now you understand basic supply and demand. Let's scale up.

=== Production Chains

The most profitable early vertical integration:

1. Energy Cells (cheapest producer, everything needs them)
2. Refined Metals (ore + energy) — used by every shipyard
3. Hull Parts (refined metals + energy) — station building bottleneck
4. Claytronics (silicon + energy) — advanced station building

Aim to control all four before expanding into weapon components or ship tech.

=== Sector Selection for Stations

When choosing where to build, prioritize:

- _Resource richness:_ High ore/silicon concentration (use Map -> Resource Filters)
- _Sunlight:_ greater than 100% for solar production
- _Safety:_ Low pirate/Xenon activity
- _Market access:_ Short travel time to faction wharfs and shipyards

== Fleet Management

=== Ship Roles

- _M class (Corvette/Frigate):_ Patrol, escort, boarding
- _L class (Destroyer/Freighter):_ Sector defence, bulk cargo
- _XL class (Carrier/Battleship):_ Fleet command, sector assault

=== Fleet Commands

Use the Map -> right-click your ship -> Fleet Commands:
- _Defend Position:_ Ship patrols a radius around a point.
- _Attack:_ Ship engages a target or target area.
- _Intercept:_ Combat ships engage enemies within range.
- _Supply Fleet:_ Resupply ammo/missiles from a designated freighter.

== Diplomacy

- _Trade licenses:_ Some factions require them to trade in their space.
- _Embassies:_ Build in faction capitals to improve relations faster.
- _Black market:_ Find station bartenders for illegal trades.
